# Text and fonts

```elixir
Mix.install([{:smith, "~> 0.3.0"}, {:kino, "~> 0.19.0"}])
```

## Inspect a real font

This example uses the bundled SIL-licensed Graduate font. Replace the path with
an explicit TTF/OTF file to choose your own typeface. Keep private rosters and
custom assets outside the public repository. FreeType, HarfBuzz and pkg-config
are native prerequisites; see the OCEx installation guide.

```elixir
alias Smith.{Font, Text, Sketch, Plane}
{:ok, font} = Font.load(Path.join(:code.priv_dir(:smith), "fonts/Graduate-Regular.ttf"))
name = "ALEXANDRA"
text = Text.new(name, font: font, size: 10, align: {:center, :center})
{:ok, layout} = Text.layout(text)
IO.inspect(Map.take(layout.report, [:font, :width, :height, :advance]))
Smith.Kino.render(layout.result, label: "Actual font outlines", view: :top)
```

## Fit without stretching

Size is measured in em millimeters, not visible capital height. Fit uses the
actual ink bounds and scales uniformly. A minimum size makes overly long names
fail for review, rather than producing arbitrarily small lettering.

```elixir
{:ok, fitted} = Text.fit(text, {40, 12}, margin: 1, min_size: 5)
{:ok, label} = Text.validate(fitted,
  within: {{-20, -6}, {20, 6}}, margin: 1, min_height: 4)
:passed = label.report.status
IO.inspect(label.report.checks)
{:ok, artifacts} = Text.write(label, "output/name-reports")
IO.puts(artifacts.report)
Smith.Kino.render(label.result, label: "Fitted name", view: :top)
```

PNG and outline SVG previews come from the same measured snapshot. The JSON
records the font SHA-256, geometry revision, glyph positions and fit checks.
These checks do not certify minimum stroke thickness or a printer's resolution.

## Build a raised-name tag

The base is 3 mm thick. The lettering starts at 2.8 mm so its extrusion overlaps
and fuses with the base. The circular left end is reserved for a sports motif.

```elixir
letters = label.result |> Smith.from_result() |> Smith.extrude({0, 0, 1})
  |> Smith.translate({8, 0, 2.8})
base = Sketch.slot(70, 22)
  |> Sketch.cut(Sketch.circle(2, at: {-29, 0}))
  |> Smith.extrude(3)
tag = base |> Smith.fuse(letters)
{:ok, result} = Smith.evaluate(tag)
{:ok, inspection} = Smith.Inspection.run(%{tag: result},
  checks: [{:topology, :tag, :solids, expected: 1}])
:passed = inspection.status
Smith.Kino.render(result, label: "Printable named tag", edges: true)
```

## Export checked print files

```elixir
{:ok, files} = Smith.export(result, "output/keychains", name: "sample-player", on_bed: true)
IO.puts(files.three_mf)
```

To batch a roster, put the fitting and modeling cells in a function taking the
name, then call it for each entry. Use unique safe export IDs rather than raw
names as filenames. To engrave, place the text cutter inside the top surface and
use `Smith.cut/2`. See the text guide for arbitrary-plane placement and limits.
